     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. A single 4,000 mg dose of ibuprofen is in the range where toxicity is expected, and 16,000 mg/day has no therapeutic use I know of. It would put the patient at serious risk of GI hemorrhage, acute kidney injury, metabolic acidosis, and CNS effects. Normal kidney and liver function and no allergies don't change that.\n\nI can enter any of these right away:\n\n- Ibuprofen 400 mg PO four times daily (1,600 mg/day)\n- Ibuprofen 600 mg PO four times daily (2,400 mg/day)\n- Ibuprofen 800 mg PO three times daily (2,400 mg/day)\n\nIf the aim is stronger analgesia or anti-inflammatory effect, I can enter a different agent or a multimodal regimen, such as scheduled acetaminophen plus ibuprofen. If you think there's an unusual clinical reason for this dose, I'd recommend talking with pharmacy or clinical toxicology before it goes into the system."
      }
     ],
